*** Add File:docs/runbooks/rollback_vwc.md
+````markdown name=docs/runbooks/rollback_vwc.md
+```text
+Rollback plan for a misconfigured ValidatingWebhookConfiguration (VWC)
+
+If a VWC is blocking workloads, follow this safe, tested process:
+
+1) Diagnose
+   - See which VWC exists:
+       kubectl get validatingwebhookconfiguration
+   - Describe it and inspect recent webhook server logs:
+       kubectl describe validatingwebhookconfiguration aegis-validating-webhook
+       kubectl -n aegis logs deploy/aegis-admission-webhook
+
+2) Non-destructive safe rollback
+   - Set failurePolicy to Ignore so the webhook continues to receive traffic but does not block requests:
+       ./ops/webhook/vwc_rollback.sh ignore aegis-validating-webhook
+   - Confirm workloads can be created again.
+
+3) Fix and test in staging
+   - Fix webhook logic / TLS / CA bundle or other issues.
+   - Test with a representative Job manifest in staging.
+
+4) Re-enable blocking mode
+   - Restore failurePolicy to Fail:
+       ./ops/webhook/vwc_rollback.sh fail aegis-validating-webhook
+
+5) Emergency delete (only if absolutely necessary)
+   - If you must restore cluster operations immediately and 'ignore' does not help:
+       ./ops/webhook/vwc_rollback.sh delete aegis-validating-webhook
+   - Re-deploy the webhook after fixing and testing in staging.
+
+Notes:
+ - Prefer 'ignore' to preserve auditing; delete only as last resort.
+ - Keep this script and this runbook accessible to on-call operators.
+
+```
