*** Add File:docs/runbooks/secureagg_and_dp.md
+````markdown name=docs/runbooks/secureagg_and_dp.md
+```text
+Secure aggregation vs Differential Privacy — recommended operational approach
+
+Context
+- Cryptographic secure aggregation (MPC / PySyft / CrypTen) provides stronger guarantees: server cannot see individual client updates.
+- Differential Privacy (DP) with client-side noise (Opacus) provides statistical privacy guarantees and is often much easier to deploy and scale.
+
+Recommendation (practical)
+1) DP-first (short term)
+   - Run privacy tuning to find acceptable noise/sample_rate (epsilon) vs utility.
+   - Use Opacus client-side clipping + noise in FL clients; require authenticated/attested clients.
+   - Record chosen DP parameters in Vault and the runbook.
+2) Benchmark secure aggregation (parallel)
+   - Use ops/secure_agg/benchmark.py to measure latency and memory for expected model sizes.
+   - If DP yields unacceptable utility or threat model requires cryptographic aggregation, plan MPC sprint.
+3) MPC integration sprint
+   - Implement PySyft/CrypTen based secure aggregator; add mTLS/auth and drop-out tolerance.
+   - Add monitoring and scaling guidance and an operator playbook for key management.
+
+Tools in repo
+- ops/secure_agg/benchmark.py  : benchmark prototype aggregator
+- .github/workflows/secureagg_benchmark.yml : CI benchmark workflow
+- ops/secure_agg/INTEGRATION_NEXT_STEPS.md : recommended sprint tasks
+
+Decision guardrails
+- Prefer DP unless:
+   - Epsilon required is too large to protect privacy, OR
+   - Strong legal/compliance requirement mandates cryptographic guarantees.
+
+````
